<h1>Security Model</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">How Gate protects your data: file permissions, atomic writes, TLS, shell injection prevention, rule signing, and audit integrity.</p>

    <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>

    <p>Gate is a security tool that handles sensitive data -- your credentials. Its own security model is designed around these principles:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">100% local.</strong> No telemetry, no cloud services, no data exfiltration. Everything runs on your machine.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Least privilege.</strong> Files are created with restrictive permissions. Network calls are limited to credential verification against provider APIs.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Defense in depth.</strong> Multiple layers of protection: file permissions, atomic writes, TLS, input sanitization, and output redaction.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="file-permissions">File Permissions</h2>

    <p>Gate uses restrictive Unix file permissions for all sensitive files it creates.</p>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Permission</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>.env</td><td>0o600</td><td>Owner read/write only. Contains extracted secret values.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>.env.enc</td><td>0o600</td><td>Owner read/write only. Encrypted vault file.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>.gate/</td><td>0o700</td><td>Owner read/write/execute only. Contains config, cache, snapshots.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>.gate/cache/</td><td>0o700</td><td>Verification cache directory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>.gate/snapshots/</td><td>0o700</td><td>Auto-fix snapshot directory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>.gate/incidents/</td><td>0o700</td><td>Incident records directory.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Audit log</td><td>0o600</td><td>Append-only audit log with integrity chain.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Purge scripts</td><td>0o700</td><td>Generated history purge scripts (executable).</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Permissions are set immediately upon file creation using <code>fs.writeFileSync</code> with the <code>mode</code> option. Gate does not create files with permissive defaults and then <code>chmod</code> them -- the permissions are correct from the start.</p>

    <h2 id="atomic-writes">Atomic File Writes</h2>

    <p>Gate never writes directly to target files. Instead, it follows the atomic write pattern:</p>

    <ol>
      <li>Write the full content to a temporary file in the same directory (e.g., <code>.env.tmp.abc123</code>).</li>
      <li>Set the correct permissions on the temporary file.</li>
      <li>Rename the temporary file to the target path using <code>fs.renameSync</code>.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>This ensures that if Gate crashes or is interrupted mid-write, the original file is not corrupted. The rename operation is atomic on all supported file systems (ext4, APFS, NTFS).</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt">// Simplified atomic write implementation</span>
<span class="kw">const</span> tmpPath = target + <span class="str">'.tmp.'</span> + randomId();
fs.writeFileSync(tmpPath, content, { mode: <span class="val">0o600</span> });
fs.renameSync(tmpPath, target);</div>

    <h2 id="tls-enforcement">TLS Enforcement</h2>

    <p>All network calls made by Gate use TLS (HTTPS). This applies to:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Credential verification API calls (16 providers)</li>
      <li>Update checks (npm registry)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Gate rejects non-TLS connections. There is no flag to disable TLS enforcement. If a provider endpoint does not support TLS, verification for that provider is skipped.</p>

    <p>Gate uses Node.js's built-in <code>https</code> module with the system certificate store. No custom certificate pinning is used, which allows Gate to work behind corporate proxies that perform TLS inspection.</p>

    <h2 id="shell-injection">Shell Injection Prevention</h2>

    <p>Gate never passes user-controlled input through a shell interpreter. All subprocess execution uses <code>execFileSync</code> with explicit argument arrays instead of <code>execSync</code> with string interpolation.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt">// CORRECT: execFileSync with argument array</span>
execFileSync(<span class="str">'git'</span>, [<span class="str">'log'</span>, <span class="str">'--oneline'</span>, <span class="str">'-n'</span>, <span class="str">'10'</span>]);

<span class="cmt">// NEVER USED: string interpolation through shell</span>
<span class="cmt">// execSync(`git log --oneline -n ${userInput}`);</span></div>

    <p>This prevents shell metacharacter injection even if file paths or git refs contain special characters like <code>;</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>$</code>, or backticks.</p>

    <h2 id="secret-redaction">Secret Redaction in Output</h2>

    <p>Gate never displays full credential values in terminal output, logs, or reports.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Terminal output:</strong> Secret values are truncated to show only the first few and last few characters (e.g., <code>sk_live_51Hb3a...R4pM</code>).</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">JSON reports:</strong> Same truncation applied to the <code>match</code> field.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">SARIF output:</strong> Redacted values in all message and location fields.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Audit log:</strong> Only the rule ID, file path, and line number are recorded. The credential value is never logged.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Error messages:</strong> Stack traces and error output are sanitized to remove any credential values that might appear in variable names or error contexts.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The only place full credential values are stored is in the <code>.env</code> file (after auto-fix) and the verification cache (as a SHA-256 hash, not the value itself).</p>

    <h2 id="rule-signing">Rule Signing (HMAC-SHA256)</h2>

    <p>Gate's 148 built-in detection rules are shipped as signed JSON files. Each rule file includes an HMAC-SHA256 signature that is verified at load time.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Signing algorithm:</strong> HMAC-SHA256</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Verification:</strong> Gate verifies the signature of each rule file before loading it. If a signature does not match, the rule is rejected and a warning is printed.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Tampering protection:</strong> This prevents malicious modification of rule files (e.g., weakening patterns to allow secrets through, or adding patterns that exfiltrate data).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt">// Rule file structure</span>
{
  <span class="kw">"rules"</span>: [ ... ],
  <span class="kw">"signature"</span>: <span class="str">"a1b2c3d4...hmac-sha256-signature"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"version"</span>: <span class="str">"2.0.0"</span>
}</div>

    <p>Custom rules defined in <code>.gaterc</code> are not signed (they are user-provided). Only the built-in rule pack is signed.</p>

    <h2 id="audit-log-integrity">Audit Log Integrity Chain (SHA-256)</h2>

    <p>Gate's audit log uses a hash chain to ensure integrity. Each log entry includes the SHA-256 hash of the previous entry, forming a tamper-evident chain.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt">// Audit log entry structure</span>
{
  <span class="kw">"timestamp"</span>: <span class="str">"2026-03-21T14:30:22Z"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"action"</span>: <span class="str">"scan"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"files"</span>: <span class="val">847</span>,
  <span class="kw">"findings"</span>: <span class="val">2</span>,
  <span class="kw">"duration"</span>: <span class="str">"1.2s"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"prevHash"</span>: <span class="str">"sha256:a1b2c3d4e5f6..."</span>,
  <span class="kw">"hash"</span>: <span class="str">"sha256:f6e5d4c3b2a1..."</span>
}</div>

    <p>Verify the integrity of the audit log at any time:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate audit --verify</span>

<span class="val">  Audit log integrity: OK</span>
<span class="dim">  Entries: 142</span>
<span class="dim">  First entry: 2026-01-15T10:00:00Z</span>
<span class="dim">  Last entry: 2026-03-21T14:30:22Z</span>
<span class="dim">  Chain: unbroken</span></div>

    <p>If any entry has been modified, added, or removed, the verification will fail and report which entries are affected.</p>

    <h2 id="gate-offline">GATE_OFFLINE Mode</h2>

    <p>Set the <code>GATE_OFFLINE=1</code> environment variable to disable all network communication. In offline mode:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Credential verification is skipped (all results marked UNKNOWN)</li>
      <li>Update checks are disabled</li>
      <li>No DNS resolution or TCP connections are made</li>
      <li>All other functionality works normally</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">GATE_OFFLINE=1 gate scan</span></div>

    <p>This is useful for air-gapped environments, CI systems without internet access, or situations where you want to ensure zero network traffic.</p>

    <h2 id="supply-chain">Supply Chain Security</h2>

    <p>Gate minimizes its dependency surface to reduce supply chain risk:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Minimal dependencies.</strong> Gate uses as few npm packages as possible. Core scanning and fixing logic is implemented from scratch.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">No native modules.</strong> Gate is pure JavaScript/TypeScript. No compiled native code, no node-gyp, no platform-specific binaries.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Signed rule packs.</strong> Detection rules are signed to prevent tampering (see above).</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Open source.</strong> All code is available for inspection on GitHub.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="vulnerability-reporting">Vulnerability Reporting</h2>

    <p>If you discover a security vulnerability in Gate, please report it responsibly:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Email: shadoe@penumbraforge.com</li>
      <li>Do not open a public GitHub issue for security vulnerabilities</li>
      <li>Include steps to reproduce and the expected impact</li>
      <li>We will respond within 48 hours and aim to patch within 7 days</li>
    </ul>
